layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | With
    a(href="/docs/autopilot") autopilot
    | on, the factory needs no coordinator: the server's conductor moves each package along the line by itself, and
    | you only answer the questions it asks you. This page is for the other case: a
    a(href="/docs/projects") project
    | with autopilot off, where a
    strong coordinator
    | (a Claude Code session in a herdr terminal, or you) drives the same steps by hand, and for supervising a project
    | that is on autopilot. Either way Buildyboy is the control tower: the coordinator reads and writes everything
    | through its MCP server, and you follow along on your phone.
  p
    | This page sets up that link, explains the consumption governor, shows the loop to run by hand with the kit's
    | steps, and shows how herdr terminals appear in Buildyboy.
  +callout(variant="info")
    @content
      p
        | Not sure which one you need? Turn autopilot on for the project and watch it with
        code factory_status
        | or the
        a(href="/docs/skills#factory-status") factory-status
        | skill. Come back here when you want to take a package over, or when a project should not run on its own.

  h2#pieces The three pieces
  dl
    dt Claude Code
    dd The agents: the workers, each with its own subagents, and a coordinator when you run one by hand.
    dt herdr (hc)
    dd The execution: terminals that stay alive, so a worker can be watched and corrected while it runs.
    dt Buildyboy
    dd The memory and the phone: projects, backlog, usage and the governor, gates, decisions and the daily digest. With autopilot on it also does the coordinating.

  h2#token Step 1 · Create an agent key
  p
    | The coordinator does not use a machine's key. It uses an
    strong agent key
    | , which can only reach the factory's tools, not your sessions' controls or settings, and which you can revoke at
    | any time. It starts nothing on your machines unless you allow it (see
    a(href="#launch") Letting agents start agents
    | ).
  ol
    li
      | Open
      strong Settings
      | and find the
      strong Factory
      | section.
    li
      | Under
      strong Agent tokens
      | , type a name such as
      code coordinator
      | and create it.
    li
      | Copy the key (it starts with
      code bba_
      | ) right away: it is shown only once. The section also shows a ready
      code claude mcp add
      | line with it filled in.
  p
    | On the server itself,
    code node cli/bb.ts agent-token coordinator
    | does the same and prints the key.

  h2#register Step 2 · Register the MCP server
  p
    | Run this once on the machine where the coordinator will run, with your server's address, your key and the folder
    | where the Buildyboy code is:
  pre
    code
      | claude mcp add buildyboy --scope user -e BUILDYBOY_URL=https://buildyboy.example -e BUILDYBOY_AGENT_TOKEN=bba_… -- node C:\path\to\buildyboy\cli\bb.ts mcp
  p
    | Claude Code then starts
    code bb mcp
    | by itself in every session, and it keeps working while the server is away (see below). Check it with
    code claude mcp list
    | ; inside a session,
    code /mcp
    | shows the buildyboy tools.
  p
    | A coordinator on a machine without the Buildyboy code can use the server's HTTP endpoint instead, with no offline
    | queue:
  pre
    code
      | claude mcp add --transport http buildyboy {{ site.url }}/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer bba_…" --scope user
  p
    | Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, Codex and scripts are covered in
    a(href="/docs/agents") Connecting agents
    | .
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | The key goes into Claude Code's own configuration file. Treat it like a password, and revoke it in Settings if
        | it leaks; a revoked key stops working at once.

  h2#tools The tools
  p
    | Each tool answers with JSON. A refused call says why, for example "task P3 waits on P1". The
    a(href="/docs/mcp-tools") MCP tool reference
    | has every argument, result and error. The planner has a tool of its own,
    code plan_request
    | (see
    a(href="/docs/planning#planner") Planning
    | ).
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Tool
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What it does
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code usage_get
          td(data-part="td") The 5-hour and 7-day windows: percent used, when each resets, and how old the data is.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code governor_get
          td(data-part="td") May a new worker start now? See the governor below.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code sessions_list, sessions_get
          td(data-part="td") Status, context, cost and branch of the sessions Buildyboy knows.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code projects_list, projects_get
          td(data-part="td") The factory projects, and one with its counts, its line and the packages ready next.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_list, tasks_get
          td(data-part="td") The backlog (all, one project, or only the packages ready to start); one package with its history, gates and requests.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_create, tasks_update
          td(data-part="td") Add a package (also from chosen findings), or change its status, branch, cost, prompt version, terminal, used migrations, note or archived state.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_claim
          td(data-part="td") Take the next ready package (or a given one, or the next of one project), mark it În lucru and get its port, database and migration range. Refused while the governor says no.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code prompts_render, prompts_lint, prompts_save
          td(data-part="td") Fill the harness template, check it, and save it as a versioned prompt tied to a package.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code steps_run
          td(data-part="td") Run one of the kit's steps for a package on its project's runner: prepare, qa, cleanup or revert. Poll it with gates_status.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code gates_record
          td(data-part="td") Record a gate result (G1 to G5) with a summary, findings and screenshots taken from local files.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code gates_run, gates_status
          td(data-part="td") Run a gate on the runner from the project's line file and get back only the verdict, summary and findings; gates_status also reports a step. The log stays in Buildyboy.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code findings_list, findings_update
          td(data-part="td") Gate findings by package, status and severity; mark them fixed, deferred, for the architect or won't fix.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code decision_ask, decision_get
          td(data-part="td") Ask you something on your phone; read your answer later.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code digest_post
          td(data-part="td") Send the digest now, with an optional note.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code control_get
          td(data-part="td") Your requests to stop a package or revert its integration, so whoever coordinates can act on them.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code factory_status
          td(data-part="td") Everything in one small answer: the governor, alerts, each project's autopilot state, the packages on the line and what waits for you. What an agent calls when autopilot is on.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code machines_list, tasks_launch, coordinator_launch
          td(data-part="td") Find a machine and its allowed folders, then start a worker for a package, or another coordinator. Off until you allow it; see Letting agents start agents below.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code decisions_list, decision_answer, decision_cancel, control_request, projects_save, projects_set, inbox_list, inbox_answer
          td(data-part="td")
            | The tools that let an agent act for you. Most need a key you made an operator; the two inbox tools need its Inbox switch. See
            a(href="/docs/operators") Operators
            | .
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code docs_get
          td(data-part="td") This manual's agent pages as Markdown, so an agent can learn the factory from inside its session.

  h2#governor The consumption governor
  p
    | Your subscription has a rolling 5-hour window and a weekly 7-day window. The governor reads them from the
    | statusline bridge and answers the question of whoever coordinates, the conductor included: can I start a worker now,
    | or until when should I wait?
  ul
    li
      strong 7-day window at 80% or more:
      | the factory stops entirely until that window resets, so the rest of the week stays for the sessions you run
      | yourself.
    li
      strong 5-hour window at 85% or more:
      | no new workers until it resets; the ones already running finish.
    li
      strong A window missing from the data
      | (for example after a plan change) is ignored.
    li
      strong Data older than 30 minutes, or none at all:
      | no new workers, and the reason says so.
  p
    | The three numbers are in Settings → Factory. The backlog page shows the governor's answer at the top, and
    code GET /api/governor
    | returns it as JSON.

  h2#by-hand The by-hand loop with the kit
  p
    | With autopilot off, nothing moves on its own: you or a coordinator session run the line's stages with the tools
    | below. It is the same sequence the
    a(href="/docs/autopilot#stages") conductor
    | follows, and the project's
    a(href="/docs/line") line file
    | decides what each step does. The
    a(href="/docs/skills#factory-foreman") factory-foreman
    | skill of the plugin (
    code /buildyboy:factory-foreman
    | ) packages this loop for Claude Code.
  ol
    li
      code governor_get
      | : start new work only when
      code canStart
      | is true. With
      code stopAll
      | , stop.
    li
      code control_get
      | : act on your stop and revert requests first, then call it again with
      code ack: true
      | .
    li
      code tasks_claim
      | with
      code project
      | set to the project's key. The package becomes În lucru and you get its port, database and migration range.
    li
      code steps_run
      | with
      code prepare
      | : the runner creates the package's worktree and branch, writes the
      code .factory/
      | folder (the package and the guard's file) and runs the line's setup command. Poll
      code gates_status
      | until it is done.
    li
      code steps_run
      | with
      code qa
      | , when the line has acceptance tests: a verifier writes the black-box tests before the code, and they are
      | committed and tagged.
    li
      code tasks_launch
      | to start the worker, or start it yourself in the worktree with
      code BB_TASK
      | set to the package and
      code BB_ROLE=lucrator
      | .
    li
      | Wait for the worker's last message. It ends with a
      code FACTORY-STATUS
      | line:
      code done
      | ,
      code question
      | or
      code blocked
      | , the last two followed by the text. A question or a block needs an answer from you or the coordinator.
    li
      code tasks_update
      | to La porți (this starts a gate round), then
      code gates_run
      | for G1, G2, G3 and G4 in order, and G5 last. After each one, poll
      code gates_status
      | until it is done. A gate the line does not enable comes back as skipped.
    li
      | On a failure, send the open findings back to the worker (a live terminal takes a short message; longer text goes
      | in a file under
      code .factory/inbox/
      | ), move the package back to În lucru and repeat from the gates.
    li
      | When G5 has passed:
      code tasks_update
      | to Integrată with the migrations the package used, then
      code steps_run
      | with
      code cleanup
      | to end its terminal and remove the worktree and the branch.
    li
      code decision_ask
      | for anything only you can decide, and read the answer later with
      code decision_get
      | . Keep working on other packages meanwhile.
  p
    | A package you move by hand while autopilot is on is picked up by the conductor from its new status, so you can
    | take one over and hand it back. See
    a(href="/docs/autopilot#by-hand") By hand
    | .

  h2#bookkeeping The factory's bookkeeping
  ul
    li
      strong Rounds:
      | every move into La porți starts a gate round. When a gate fails in the last allowed round (3 by default,
      | Settings → Factory), the package becomes Blocată and you get one decision: another round, another round for
      | one gate only, or stop the package. Moving it back to În lucru with a note allows one more round.
    li
      strong Cost:
      | sessions started with
      code BB_TASK
      | and
      code BB_ROLE
      | (lucrator, qa, poarta, coordonator) are linked to the package, as are its herdr terminal and its job. The
      | package page shows their exact sum, split by role.
    li
      strong Gates on the runner:
      | the runner reads the project's line file,
      code factory.json
      | , in its main checkout, never in the worker's worktree (see
      a(href="/docs/line") The line
      | ).
      code gates_run
      | sends one gate to the runner. If the line has argv steps for that gate the runner runs them without a shell;
      | otherwise it runs the kit's built-in gate. It reports the verdict, summary and findings, and the log stays on
      | the package page. The settings that used to be kept per repo in Settings → Factory now live on the project and in
      | its line file.
    li
      strong Resources:
      code tasks_claim
      | gives each package a port, a database name and a range of migration numbers, from the line file's
      code resources
      | or, for a project that came from the old per-repo settings, from its own config. When a
      | package is integrated with migrations inside another active package's range, that package and the digest get
      | a warning to renumber.
    li
      strong Findings
      | are kept with a status. A later pass of the same gate marks its blockers and majors fixed; minors stay open
      | until you pick some on the backlog and make a package from them.
    li
      strong When the server is away,
      code bb mcp
      | queues gates_record, tasks_update, digest_post and prompts_save in
      code ~/.buildyboy/outbox.jsonl
      | and replays them once it is back, each exactly once. Reads return the last known answer marked stale, and
      | governor_get is computed from the local statusline sample. With
      code ntfyTopic
      | in
      code ~/.buildyboy/config.json
      | you get one phone message after 10 minutes without the server, and one when it is back.

  h2#herdr herdr terminals
  p
    | When a machine's
    a(href="/docs/runner") runner
    | finds
    code hc
    | , it reports herdr's terminals every 15 seconds, and the
    a(href="/docs/floor") Floor
    | lists them with their status. A terminal is linked to the Claude Code session running inside it, and to its
    | package when it was started for one.
  p
    | The runner looks for
    code hc
    | in this order:
    code hcPath
    | in
    code runner.json
    | , the
    code BUILDYBOY_HC
    | variable, herdr-core's usual install folder on Windows, then your PATH. Set either one to
    code off
    | to turn the integration off.
  p
    | From a package's page you can also start it as an interactive agent in a new herdr terminal, rather than as a
    | one-shot job: see
    a(href="/docs/backlog#launch") Starting a package
    | .

  h2#launch Letting agents start agents
  p
    | A coordinator can start workers for its packages, and even other coordinators, through a machine's runner,
    | the same way the package page does: inside the runner's allowed folders, with checked options, and claiming the
    | package. It is off for every key until you turn it on.
  ol
    li
      | In
      strong Settings → Factory → Agent tokens
      | , set
      strong May launch
      | for that key to
      strong Workers
      | (the
      code tasks_launch
      | tool) or
      strong Workers and coordinators
      | (also
      code coordinator_launch
      | ).
    li
      | Leave
      strong Ask me before each launch
      | on to approve every launch from your phone. The agent's first call puts a question in
      a(href="/docs/backlog#decisions") Decisions
      | ; nothing starts until you answer
      strong Launch
      | and the agent asks again. An approval is good for one launch, for 24 hours.
  p
    | A launch is refused while the
    a(href="#governor") governor
    | says no new work may start, and each one is in the audit log under the key's name. A coordinator started this way
    | runs in a herdr terminal named
    code bb-coord-…
    | and reads its instructions from Buildyboy, so the machine needs the MCP server registered for Claude Code (step 2).
    | The agent finds machines and their allowed folders with
    code machines_list
    | .

  h2#activate Turning it on for this machine
  p The governor needs usage data, and nothing in Buildyboy edits Claude Code's settings for you. On the machine where the coordinator runs:
  ol
    li
      | Pair the machine and install the plugin, as in
      a(href="/docs/machines") Pairing machines
      | .
    li
      | Add the statusline bridge to
      code ~/.claude/settings.json
      | . This is where the usage windows come from:
      pre
        code
          | "statusLine": { "type": "command", "command": "node C:/path/to/buildyboy/plugin/statusline/bb-statusline.mjs" }
      | If you already have a status line, put its command in the
      code BUILDYBOY_STATUSLINE_NEXT
      | variable and the bridge runs it after itself.
    li
      | Optionally paste the OTEL block from Settings → Snippets into the
      code env
      | section of the same file, for exact costs and tokens.
    li
      | Create an agent key and register the MCP server (steps 1 and 2 above).
    li
      | Run the doctor with the key set. Its
      strong factory
      | block lists exactly what is still missing, with the next step for each:
      pre
        code
          | $env:BUILDYBOY_AGENT_TOKEN='bba_…'; node cli/bb.ts doctor
    li Start an interactive claude session (the status line only runs there), wait a minute, and check that the backlog page shows fresh usage.
